#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# intersection

#% text
# `intersection(xs, ys, ...)` gives the elements that occur in every one of the lists, without repetitions, in canonical order.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The result is sorted and has no repeated elements.

#% item
# Elements are compared by identity.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
intersection([1, 2, 3, 4], [3, 4, 5])
#% output
#= [3, 4]

#% cell
intersection([a, b, c], [b, c, d], [c, b])
#% output
#= [b, c]

#% cell
intersection([1, 2], [3])
#% output
#= []

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# `same_test` decides when two elements are the same:

#% cell
intersection([1, 2, 3], [-2, -3], same_test: (a, b) => abs(a) == abs(b))
#% output
#= [2, 3]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The common divisors of two numbers, and the greatest of them:

#% cell
cd = intersection(divisors(48), divisors(180))
[cd, max(cd)]
#% output
#= [[1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12], 12]

#% text
# Letters that two words share:

#% cell
intersection(characters("listen"), characters("silent"))
#% output
#= ["e", "i", "l", "n", "s", "t"]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The greatest common divisor is the largest common divisor:

#% cell
gcd(48, 180)
#% output
#= 12

#% text
# `complement` gives what is in the first list and not in the others:

#% cell
complement([1, 2, 3, 4], [3, 4, 5])
#% output
#= [1, 2]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `union`, `complement`, `is_subset`, `member_of`, `gcd`
